1
00:00:00.333 --> 00:00:04.738
So now that you've added some evaluations
to your router and skills for your agent,

2
00:00:05.171 --> 00:00:07.474
the next step is to evaluate the path

3
00:00:07.474 --> 00:00:10.677
or understand if it's taking an efficient
path for a given query.

4
00:00:11.144 --> 00:00:13.313
So in order to do that, again,
you're going to start

5
00:00:13.313 --> 00:00:16.316
by importing a few libraries
that will be useful here.

6
00:00:16.416 --> 00:00:18.785
Most of the things
you've already seen for Phoenix.

7
00:00:18.785 --> 00:00:20.720
And then now you've got some new things
from Phoenix

8
00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:23.857
for what's called experiments, which
you'll learn about in just a second here.

9
00:00:24.657 --> 00:00:27.660
So you can import those libraries.
And one other thing to call out

10
00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:32.265
is that, again, the agent's been imported
from your utils method here.

11
00:00:32.265 --> 00:00:35.301
So, you have the same agent that you've
been working with the whole time.

12
00:00:35.435 --> 00:00:37.037
You're importing that run agent method.

13
00:00:37.037 --> 00:00:38.972
So just know that that code
is sitting in the background.

14
00:00:40.073 --> 00:00:41.074
And then

15
00:00:41.074 --> 00:00:44.244
here you're still going
to need to connect to the Phoenix client.

16
00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:47.347
So in this case I'll just save that
Phoenix client variable.

17
00:00:48.281 --> 00:00:51.818
And then the way that you can evaluate
your trajectory as you learned

18
00:00:51.818 --> 00:00:55.622
in the previous slides, is by running
a set of queries through your agent

19
00:00:55.922 --> 00:00:59.559
and then tracing the number of steps
taken by each of those queries.

20
00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:03.029
So in order to evaluate the trajectory
here,

21
00:01:03.029 --> 00:01:06.399
you have to actually compare multiple
runs of the agent together.

22
00:01:06.499 --> 00:01:07.367
So in order to do

23
00:01:07.367 --> 00:01:10.336
that, you're going to use a tool
called a run experiment within Phoenix,

24
00:01:10.537 --> 00:01:13.540
which allows you to run
multiple different runs of your agent

25
00:01:13.606 --> 00:01:16.576
and then compare those
to each other in certain ways.

26
00:01:16.776 --> 00:01:20.680
So here's a good point to pause
and dive a little more into experiments

27
00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:21.281
and how they work.

28
00:01:22.449 --> 00:01:25.085
Experiments in Phoenix
are made up of a few different steps.

29
00:01:25.085 --> 00:01:28.054
First, it's taking a data
set of different test cases,

30
00:01:28.388 --> 00:01:32.892
then sending those into a particular task
or job to run

31
00:01:33.359 --> 00:01:36.129
and then evaluating
the results of that task.

32
00:01:36.129 --> 00:01:39.732
So a data set of test cases
is going to have a bunch of different

33
00:01:40.033 --> 00:01:42.836
queries or questions
that you might run through your agent.

34
00:01:42.836 --> 00:01:45.371
And typically you'll have an input value
inside of those.

35
00:01:45.371 --> 00:01:48.374
examples
as well as maybe an expected output.

36
00:01:48.475 --> 00:01:50.376
In this first case,
you won't have an expected output.

37
00:01:50.376 --> 00:01:51.644
You'll just have an input.

38
00:01:51.644 --> 00:01:54.214
Sometimes you have expected outputs,
sometimes you don't.

39
00:01:54.214 --> 00:01:57.217
And that dictates some of the evaluators
you can use later on.

40
00:01:57.517 --> 00:01:59.552
So you have a set of examples here.

41
00:01:59.552 --> 00:02:02.455
And then you'll run those
through a version of your agent.

42
00:02:02.455 --> 00:02:05.458
So in this case just going to use
one single version of your agent.

43
00:02:05.625 --> 00:02:08.628
You'll get the outputs
from that particular round.

44
00:02:09.129 --> 00:02:12.699
And you'll see actually that you made
some slight modifications to your agent

45
00:02:12.699 --> 00:02:16.002
to actually track the number of steps
taken as well as part of that task.

46
00:02:16.803 --> 00:02:19.038
So you can define the task
for your experiment.

47
00:02:19.038 --> 00:02:22.442
And then once you've collected
all the results of each example

48
00:02:22.442 --> 00:02:25.845
through your task, you can
then send those to a set of evaluators.

49
00:02:26.346 --> 00:02:29.249
And those evaluators could be
the ones you set up in previous rounds.

50
00:02:29.249 --> 00:02:32.252
Or in this case you can do
more of a comparative evaluator,

51
00:02:32.552 --> 00:02:34.854
comparing different runs of your agent.

52
00:02:34.854 --> 00:02:36.122
And so in this case,

53
00:02:36.122 --> 00:02:38.391
there's another variable
that will be added to each example,

54
00:02:38.391 --> 00:02:42.228
which is the output of that
particular example through the task.

55
00:02:42.896 --> 00:02:45.331
And you'll learn a lot more
about experiments in the next lesson

56
00:02:45.331 --> 00:02:46.232
as well too.

57
00:02:46.232 --> 00:02:48.434
This is sort of
just your primer for them in this case.

58
00:02:49.502 --> 00:02:51.004
So step one there

59
00:02:51.004 --> 00:02:54.007
is creating a dataset of test cases.

60
00:02:54.741 --> 00:02:57.744
So the way that you can do that
is you have a set of questions.

61
00:02:58.144 --> 00:02:59.779
In this case convergence questions.

62
00:02:59.779 --> 00:03:01.414
And if you think back to the slides,

63
00:03:01.414 --> 00:03:02.982
one of the ways that you test
for convergence

64
00:03:02.982 --> 00:03:06.286
here is you send a lot of different
variations of a similar

65
00:03:06.286 --> 00:03:09.489
type of query through your agent
and then track the number of steps taken.

66
00:03:09.956 --> 00:03:12.559
So notice
that each of these different examples here

67
00:03:12.559 --> 00:03:16.362
is all about the number or the average
quantity of items per transaction.

68
00:03:16.396 --> 00:03:20.934
So you see the average quantity sold per
transaction mean number of items for sale.

69
00:03:21.167 --> 00:03:23.369
Calculate
the typical quantity for transactions.

70
00:03:23.369 --> 00:03:26.372
You see these are all kind of variations
on the same question.

71
00:03:26.506 --> 00:03:28.541
So the agent should take the same path
for each of these.

72
00:03:28.541 --> 00:03:30.043
But sometimes there's variation.

73
00:03:30.977 --> 00:03:33.613
So you can take this list of questions,
create a data

74
00:03:33.613 --> 00:03:36.816
frame out of them and then upload
that data frame into Phoenix.

75
00:03:37.584 --> 00:03:40.253
So now you have a data
set that lives in Phoenix.

76
00:03:40.253 --> 00:03:43.256
If you want to,
you can quickly visualize it here

77
00:03:43.656 --> 00:03:44.958
Now, if you want to look in your Phoenix

78
00:03:44.958 --> 00:03:47.961
window, you can actually see
under the datasets tab here,

79
00:03:48.094 --> 00:03:51.097
you'll now have an entry for the data
set that you have just uploaded.

80
00:03:51.164 --> 00:03:52.031
You can click into that.

81
00:03:52.031 --> 00:03:55.034
And you can see all of the different
examples that you've uploaded.

82
00:03:55.435 --> 00:03:58.438
And now you can use this data
set to start running experiments.

83
00:03:58.838 --> 00:04:01.874
So for the next step here you have to
define the task for your agent.

84
00:04:02.609 --> 00:04:05.411
Now you could just run these examples
through your agent.

85
00:04:05.411 --> 00:04:08.815
But again you want to have some count
of the number of steps that were taken.

86
00:04:08.848 --> 00:04:10.617
You might want to format
some of the messages

87
00:04:10.617 --> 00:04:13.086
so you can make slight tweaks
to your agent as well.

88
00:04:13.086 --> 00:04:15.054
When you're setting it up as a task.

89
00:04:15.054 --> 00:04:17.690
So in this case, I do a couple of things
here.

90
00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:21.527
One is actually create
a method that will format

91
00:04:21.527 --> 00:04:24.530
some of the message steps
which you can come back to in a second.

92
00:04:24.631 --> 00:04:29.435
And then you'll create a task here
that is run agent and track path.

93
00:04:29.502 --> 00:04:32.972
So starting with this task
you'll see it takes in an example.

94
00:04:33.006 --> 00:04:36.009
So if you remember each row of that data
set is an example.

95
00:04:36.342 --> 00:04:39.379
So you can see in this case
you're taking in an example variable.

96
00:04:39.846 --> 00:04:41.014
And then it's calling.

97
00:04:41.014 --> 00:04:43.683
It's taking the input value
from that example.

98
00:04:43.683 --> 00:04:46.919
And then calling the run agent method
on that particular example.

99
00:04:47.353 --> 00:04:50.089
And then finally calling
that format messages step.

100
00:04:50.089 --> 00:04:53.459
So the return from this run
agent and track path, it's going to be

101
00:04:53.459 --> 00:04:57.096
the length of the messages
inside the agent as the path length.

102
00:04:57.630 --> 00:05:00.133
And then the actual message is object.

103
00:05:00.133 --> 00:05:03.369
And then this format message step really
just is going to go

104
00:05:03.369 --> 00:05:06.372
through all of the messages
in your agent's log.

105
00:05:06.572 --> 00:05:09.575
And it's going to format them in
a little bit easier to read sort of way.

106
00:05:09.575 --> 00:05:12.312
So you can see the two calls that are made
and make it a little bit easier to

107
00:05:12.312 --> 00:05:13.046
compare here.

108
00:05:14.013 --> 00:05:16.449
So now with your task ready

109
00:05:16.449 --> 00:05:19.452
and your data set defined,
you can start an experiment.

110
00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:23.956
So to do that
you'll call this run experiment method.

111
00:05:24.624 --> 00:05:28.261
And then what this will do is it'll take
in your data set as well as your task

112
00:05:28.261 --> 00:05:31.364
or your function that gets applied
to each row of that data set.

113
00:05:32.198 --> 00:05:33.466
And then you can come up with a name.

114
00:05:33.466 --> 00:05:36.436
So in this case convergence eval
and a description.

115
00:05:36.436 --> 00:05:39.772
So if you run that now,
each row of your dataset

116
00:05:39.772 --> 00:05:42.842
will be run through the run
agent and track path method.

117
00:05:43.142 --> 00:05:44.610
And you'll get results back.

118
00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:47.747
That'll take a second to run because
it's equivalent to 17 runs of your agent.

119
00:05:47.747 --> 00:05:50.383
So give that a second to complete.

120
00:05:50.383 --> 00:05:52.552
Now you should have some results
that look like this.

121
00:05:52.552 --> 00:05:54.220
You'll see all those runs completed.

122
00:05:54.220 --> 00:05:56.089
And you can actually click
and see the results

123
00:05:56.089 --> 00:05:58.191
of that experiment inside of Phoenix.

124
00:05:58.191 --> 00:06:02.662
So if you click into that,
what you'll see is in your dataset,

125
00:06:03.029 --> 00:06:05.365
you now have an entry for experiments.

126
00:06:05.365 --> 00:06:07.266
And you have this first run
of your experiment

127
00:06:07.266 --> 00:06:09.302
that you named convergence eval.

128
00:06:09.302 --> 00:06:12.438
And you can actually click and see
all of the outputs for you to run there.

129
00:06:13.639 --> 00:06:13.873
In this

130
00:06:13.873 --> 00:06:16.876
case looks fairly successful
on each of our runs.

131
00:06:17.076 --> 00:06:20.546
And so now what you can do is
you can actually go back into the code

132
00:06:20.546 --> 00:06:24.417
and you can apply evaluators to each of
those different experiment runs.

133
00:06:24.684 --> 00:06:28.821
And so here's where you can essentially
implement your convergence evaluator.

134
00:06:29.355 --> 00:06:32.658
And so because you just run
your experiment, you have written that

135
00:06:32.658 --> 00:06:36.696
experiment has something that
you can apply and access in your code.

136
00:06:36.963 --> 00:06:39.399
So you can always view the results
of that experiment as a data frame

137
00:06:39.399 --> 00:06:42.702
where you have the output, input
and other various columns there.

138
00:06:43.369 --> 00:06:44.237
And so that's one thing

139
00:06:44.237 --> 00:06:47.240
that you can actually use
to run your convergence eval as well.

140
00:06:47.306 --> 00:06:47.874
And so

141
00:06:47.874 --> 00:06:51.644
in order to calculate the convergence
first you need to calculate the minimum

142
00:06:51.644 --> 00:06:54.647
number of steps that was taken
by all the runs of your agent.

143
00:06:54.714 --> 00:06:57.984
And so to do that add in code here

144
00:06:58.351 --> 00:07:01.821
to first take your experiment as a data
frame like you've just done above.

145
00:07:02.155 --> 00:07:05.425
And then look at the output column
and turn all of those different,

146
00:07:05.691 --> 00:07:10.329
variables into values that you can access,
and then calculate the minimum

147
00:07:10.329 --> 00:07:14.700
or optimal path by using the minimum
function on the path length

148
00:07:14.867 --> 00:07:17.303
variable
within each of those different outputs.

149
00:07:17.303 --> 00:07:21.073
So this is just going to give you a number
for the minimum path length taken.

150
00:07:22.775 --> 00:07:23.776
So if you run that you should get

151
00:07:23.776 --> 00:07:27.580
something says the optimal path length is
you should probably see five in here.

152
00:07:27.580 --> 00:07:30.516
That's a that is the optimal path length
that's being used.

153
00:07:30.516 --> 00:07:32.919
And one important thing to note
is just the way that this has been set up

154
00:07:32.919 --> 00:07:34.053
so far is it's counting.

155
00:07:34.053 --> 00:07:35.822
Every message has the path length.

156
00:07:35.822 --> 00:07:38.858
So it is including things like
the system message and the user message.

157
00:07:39.292 --> 00:07:43.062
That's fine in this case because you're
comparing a bunch of different examples

158
00:07:43.062 --> 00:07:46.065
that all include both those variables
or both those messages.

159
00:07:46.365 --> 00:07:47.767
You just want to make sure
that you're consistent.

160
00:07:47.767 --> 00:07:50.736
So if you include the user message
in the system message again that's fine.

161
00:07:50.736 --> 00:07:54.073
You just got to make sure that you do it
on every example that you're testing with.

162
00:07:55.074 --> 00:07:55.608
So now you can

163
00:07:55.608 --> 00:07:58.611
create a method to use as your evaluator.

164
00:07:59.045 --> 00:08:03.149
And in this case you can use
this, evaluate path length method

165
00:08:03.249 --> 00:08:07.186
which is going to take an output
and compare it, the path length of that

166
00:08:07.186 --> 00:08:11.591
output to the minimum or optimal
path length that you calculated earlier.

167
00:08:12.291 --> 00:08:15.828
You're also you're going to use this
create evaluator decorator here.

168
00:08:15.828 --> 00:08:16.963
This is totally optional

169
00:08:16.963 --> 00:08:21.000
but it allows you to name the evaluator
that you're going to run here and see.

170
00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:24.003
Or it'll mark
how it shows up inside of Phoenix.

171
00:08:24.504 --> 00:08:27.940
And so now you can take the experiment
that you've already run

172
00:08:28.374 --> 00:08:32.078
and use this evaluate experiment
method to take in the experiment.

173
00:08:32.245 --> 00:08:35.214
And then this evaluate path length
method that you run above.

174
00:08:35.248 --> 00:08:38.584
And that will take all of the results
from your experiment and run them through

175
00:08:38.618 --> 00:08:40.786
any evaluators that you add in here.

176
00:08:40.786 --> 00:08:42.154
AKA in this case,

177
00:08:42.154 --> 00:08:45.658
the evaluate path length and it will give
you a score at the end of it.

178
00:08:47.126 --> 00:08:47.527
That'll go

179
00:08:47.527 --> 00:08:50.530
pretty quick
because it's just a basic code-based eval.

180
00:08:50.563 --> 00:08:53.566
And if you jump over into Phoenix now,

181
00:08:53.799 --> 00:08:57.036
you'll see that your experiment,
if you go back to your dataset,

182
00:08:57.436 --> 00:09:00.806
you'll now have a column here
for convergence eval.

183
00:09:01.274 --> 00:09:03.976
It's named that because of the decorator
that you attached.

184
00:09:03.976 --> 00:09:06.979
And in this case
we've got a perfect score of one.

185
00:09:06.979 --> 00:09:09.215
Our agent has taken the correct path.

186
00:09:09.215 --> 00:09:10.616
Every example here.

187
00:09:10.616 --> 00:09:12.451
You might see a different value here.

188
00:09:12.451 --> 00:09:14.520
Every time we've run
this we've gotten a different value.

189
00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:17.390
So you may see a different value
here for one.

190
00:09:17.390 --> 00:09:18.257
And that'll give you an idea

191
00:09:18.257 --> 00:09:21.561
of whether or not your agent
is converging towards the correct path.

